{
  "dom_id": "step-q345385",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040771/assets/d43d7419f631b479478641ac203c6816693c68ffc40ccc1d4a8a72da0a03461a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{x}{3}){|}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-arccos\\,(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$",
